From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 17:47:33 -0500
Subject: feat: tidy names, important updates, folder icons in sync (S-398,
 S-406, S-393, S-402)

Tidy names (docs/features/tabs.md R65, sidebar-panels.md Pn17, settings
T52, T53). Two switches in Settings > Stedding, both off by default. "Tidy
the titles of pinned tabs" (Tabs card) shows a pinned tab's row without the
site's name, unread counts and account: "Inbox (3) - you@x.com - Gmail"
reads "Inbox". "Tidy the names of downloaded files" (Pages and menus) saves
a file whose name says nothing ("download (3).pdf", a hash, a long number)
under its page's title. A name the user gave a tab always wins; the page,
its history and the tooltip keep the whole title; a name typed in Save As
stands. The rules run on the device; nothing is sent anywhere.

An important update (docs/features/updates.md Up23, Up24; ADR 0031). A
feed item can carry Sparkle's <sparkle:criticalUpdate/> and Stedding's
<stedding:important> with a reason in one line (tooling/appcast.py
--important). Offered one, the sidebar's update pill turns red and reads
"Important Update", its tooltip the reason; and once per launch, in the
first normal window, a bar under the page says "An important update to
Stedding is ready: <reason>" with Restart Now, or Update Now while it still
has to download. Nothing restarts by itself. On Windows the reason is kept
in local state, so a launch before the update is installed still says why.

Folder icons that travel (docs/features/folders.md F42). A folder's icon
goes with it in the sidebar file, in a "stedding" key Arc ignores, and in
sidebar sync, as the record's icon field: an icon chosen on one computer
reaches the others, and an import brings it back.

A site's device picker (docs/features/site-controls.md; GitHub issue 73). A
browser test with a fake serial port asks from a click and sees the picker
open. It did not reproduce the report, and it keeps the picker from going
quiet later.

Why: tidy names were asked for and promised for this release (the owner,
2026-11-02: as local rules, never a model or a request, ADR 0020); a
release that matters must be hard to miss while the person decides when to
restart (the owner, 2026-11-03); issue 73 named the picker's path.
Removable when: never as features; the device picker test goes once
Chromium's own tests cover the chooser in Stedding's window layouts.
